    "summary": "Acoustic opener + fiery electric set with Mason's Children debut",
    "blurb": "An impromptu acoustic mini‑set opens the night after Phil is stuck in traffic, followed by a high‑energy electric second set. Highlights include the first performance of \"Mason's Children\" and scorching versions of \"Cumberland Blues,\" \"Hard To Handle,\" and \"The Other One.\" The soundboard captures the band in top form.",
    "review": "On December 19, 1969 at the Fillmore Auditorium the Dead delivered a unique two‑part experience. With Phil Lesh delayed, Bob Weir and Jerry Garcia slipped into an acoustic opener that featured “Monkey And The Engineer,” “Little Sadie,” “Long Black Limousine,” and “I've Been All Around This World.” The clean, balanced board capture lets Jerry’s lead work shine – even a playful tease of “Wake Up Little Susie” and hints of pedal steel on later numbers. Once the full band assembled, they launched into an electric blaze: “Mason's Children” made its debut, then the group rode through a seamless flow of classics—“China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider,” “Black Peter,” a rollicking “Hard To Handle,” and a tight “Cumberland Blues.” The second‑set climax featured a powerful “The Other One” sandwich between two “Cryptical Envelopment” sections, ending with the joyous “Turn On Your Lovelight.” Across three excellent soundboard sources, reviewers agree the performance is both historically significant and musically thrilling.",
